      "level": 1,
      "content": "Every Element is a Box"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Every HTML element on a webpage is treated as a rectangular box. You can easily observe this by adding a background to the elements in your code."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<div>\n2    <h3>Scarface</h3>\n3    <span>Say hello to my little friend.</span>\n4</div>"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "css",
      "content": "1div {\n2    background: #cccccc;\n3}\n4\n5h3 {\n6    background: #32a881;\n7}\n8\n9span {\n10    background: #7e82a6;\n11}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Browser Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Every Element is a Box",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As you can see, every HTML element (like <div>, <h3>, and <span>) forms a rectangular box."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "By controlling the size and space within and between these boxes, you can build a complex layout. The box model helps you do exactly that."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
